## 50K followers feels like success. Until it isn't.

Likes. Comments. An inbox full of "price please?" A busy Facebook page feels like a real business. For plenty of small businesses, Facebook *is* the store. Product photos in albums. Orders in Messenger. Payment on delivery.

It works. Until the day it doesn't. And when a Facebook-only business breaks, there is no backup. No website to fall back on. No email list to reach customers. No Google presence catching new buyers. Everything lives inside one account you don't control.

## The uncomfortable truth: you don't own your Facebook page

Rented land. Facebook sets the rules, and the rules keep changing:

- **Reach keeps shrinking.** Years ago a post reached most followers. Today organic reach sits in the single digits. Everyone else sees you only if you pay to boost.
- **Pages get restricted without warning.** Automated flags. Mistaken policy hits. A hacked admin account. Any of these can unpublish your page overnight. Appeals take weeks. Replies are automated.
- **Your audience can't move.** Try exporting follower contact details. You can't. If the page goes, the audience goes with it.

A website flips this completely. Your domain. Your content. Your customer list. Nobody throttles it. Nobody takes it down.

## Customers Google you before they buy

Here's what buyers actually do. They see your page. Then they open Google and search your business name. What do they find?

For a Facebook-only business: maybe your page. Maybe a competitor. Maybe nothing. No website means no controlled first impression. No prices. No address. No policies. No reviews page you own.

A simple website fixes this in the most boring, effective way possible. Your name on Google leads somewhere you control. Everything a hesitant buyer needs. What you sell. How to order. How to reach you. Why you're trustworthy.

## What a Facebook page can't do (but a website can)

Facebook is great at discovery. Bad at everything after that:

| You need...                | Facebook page | Your own website |
| -------------------------- | ------------- | ---------------- |
| Show up on Google          | No            | Yes              |
| Full product catalog       | Albums only   | Yes              |
| Your own checkout flow     | No            | Yes              |
| Payment gateway approval   | Very hard     | Yes              |
| Business email address     | Impossible    | Yes              |
| Customer data you own      | No            | Yes              |
| Policy pages buyers read   | Buried posts  | Yes              |

Two rows deserve emphasis. **Payment gateways and business verification** routinely demand a real website plus business email. Gmail plus a Facebook page gets rejected. And a **branded address** (`hello@yourbusiness.com`) is impossible without a domain, the same purchase a website starts with. More on that: [why your business needs email on its own domain](/blog/why-business-email).

## "But my page gets sales. Why change anything?"

Keep the page. Seriously. Facebook is where your customers hang out, and Messenger closes sales fine.

The argument was never Facebook *versus* website. It's **Facebook *plus* website**. The page brings discovery. The website brings trust, search traffic, ownership. Treat Facebook as the front window and the website as the actual store. When the algorithm squeezes reach, and it will, the store keeps running.

## What you actually need (less than you think)

Not a big portal. Not an app. Three things:

1. **A domain.** `yourbusiness.com`. Cheap, yearly, and the foundation for everything else.
2. **A simple website.** Who you are, what you sell, how to order, how to contact you. One good page beats a Facebook-only presence.
3. **A business email on that domain.** Invoices, gateway applications, customer replies. All from `you@yourbusiness.com`, never a Gmail address.

Most businesses get all three live within days, with nothing technical on their end. That's what our [email service](/email-service) covers on the email side. Same no-hassle approach gets the domain and site up.

## FAQ

### Do I need a website if my Facebook page already gets sales?

Yes. For protection and growth both. Today's sales don't survive tomorrow's restriction. And every buyer who Googles you and finds no website trusts you a little less.

### How much does a small business website cost?

Less than owners assume. A domain costs little per year. A simple one-page site is a one-time setup, not a monthly burden. The expensive option is losing your only sales channel.

### Can I keep selling through Facebook after getting a website?

Absolutely. Keep posting. Keep answering Messenger. Keep boosting. The website becomes the place your page points to. Link it in the action button, the about section, every pinned post.

### What should come first: website, domain, or business email?

The domain. Everything hangs off it. Then website and email go up in parallel, usually within a day or two.
